{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3268","artifact_type":"feature-context","name":"plan/feature-context-groom-swarm-dispatch-contract.md@sha256:9b93f8df9ef155f68dbc73715bf8e81bccf66a7f9c499a4ff190173fe2f2da7b"},"owner_reference":"3268","content":"# Feature Context \u2014 Groom-Swarm Dispatch Contract Consistency (#3268)\n\n**Artifact type**: feature-context (WHAT / WHY)\n**Item**: #3268 \u2014 `type:bug`, P2, classified `defect`\n**Scope note**: Problem space and outcomes only. No implementation approach, file edits, or rule\nwording is prescribed here. Not a replacement/migration of a local module to an external tool, so\nno Replacement Coverage Analysis applies.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Problem Space\n\n### 1.1 The observable symptom\n\nAn orchestrator running the grooming swarm has no single, consistent answer to the question\n*\"what do I put in a dispatch prompt?\"* Two teammates in the same swarm answer it in opposite ways,\nand the workflow document that instructs the orchestrator contradicts itself between its prose and\nits own diagram.\n\nVerified directly against the working tree:\n\n| Groom-swarm teammate | Declared `## Input` | Self-fetches item content? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `impact-analyst` | `selector` only | Yes \u2014 `agents/impact-analyst.md:26` |\n| `classifier` | `item_ref` only | Yes \u2014 `agents/classifier.md:25` (Phase 1) |\n| `rtica-assessor` | `item_ref` | Yes \u2014 `agents/rtica-assessor.md:39`, re-reads at `:92` |\n| `alignment-analyst` | `selector` only | Yes \u2014 `agents/alignment-analyst.md:100` |\n| `backlog-item-groomer` | **literal content fields** \u2014 item title, item description, research questions, RT-ICA summary (`agents/backlog-item-groomer.md:48-53`) | **No** \u2014 zero `backlog_view` occurrences in the file |\n| `fact-checker` | **pasted claim block** \u2014 `CLAIM`, `SOURCE_FILE`, `PRIMARY_SOURCE`, `VERIFICATION_METHOD`, `FALSIFICATION_CRITERIA` (`agents/fact-checker.md:45-55`) | **No** \u2014 zero `backlog_view` occurrences in the file |\n\nNeither non-fetching agent lacks the capability: `backlog-item-groomer.md:4` and\n`fact-checker.md:4` both declare `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog` in `tools:`.\n\nThe orchestrator-facing document reinforces the inconsistency and also disagrees with itself:\n\n- `skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/swarm.md:269-272` instructs the orchestrator\n  to paste eleven content fields into the groomer prompt (title, description, source, priority,\n  plan address, RT-ICA assessment, Fact-Check verdicts, Issue Classification, Root-Cause Analysis,\n  Impact Radius, Research section).\n- The **same file's** sequence diagram at line 94 says `GR->>GR: read all sections written by other\n  teammates` \u2014 i.e. the groomer fetches for itself. One document, two contradictory contracts.\n- Diagram lines 65-68 (`spawn (item details, ...)`, `spawn (item description)`) describe pasted\n  content for four teammates whose own files unambiguously self-fetch.\n\n### 1.2 The same shape recurs outside grooming\n\n`skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/close/start.md:107-121` has the orchestrator parse\nthe Acceptance Criteria section out of a `backlog_view` response, then re-type item title, plan\naddress, and each criterion individually into a `dh:task-worker` prompt \u2014 an agent that already\ncarries full backlog MCP access.\n\n### 1.3 The less visible half: nothing bounds *extra* framing\n\nBeyond required inputs, no dh document tells an orchestrator what it must **not** add. During the\noriginating run (`/dh:work-backlog-item groom #3169`), the orchestrator wrote *\"this is very likely\n`defect` \u2026 confirm this classification yourself\"* into a classifier dispatch. The output was not\ncorrupted, but only because the classifier's 5-whys procedure happened to generate independently\ncomputed evidence. The failure mode is undetectable after the fact: a biased section and an\nindependent section are textually indistinguishable.\n\n### 1.4 The stated root cause is only partly correct \u2014 a correction that reshapes the problem\n\nThe item's Root-Cause Analysis asserts *\"there is no documented rule for what belongs in a dynamic\nsubagent prompt.\"* **This is refuted by primary source in this repository.** The rule exists, in a\nsibling plugin:\n\n- `plugins/agent-orchestration/skills/agent-orchestration/SKILL.md:75-80` \u2014 *\"Pass-through\n  (correct) \u2014 data already in your context \u2026 Pre-gathering (incorrect) \u2014 DO NOT run commands to\n  collect data for the agent \u2026 Reason: Pre-gathering wastes context, duplicates agent work, causes\n  context rot.\"*\n- Same file, line 73 \u2014 *\"You do not use hedging language or speculation such as \u2014 'I think',\n  'probably', **'likely'**, 'seems'.\"*\n- `plugins/agent-orchestration/skills/delegate/SKILL.md:134` \u2014 *\"Do NOT read, grep, or glob files to\n  find context for the agent \u2014 the agent has full tool access and an empty context window; it does\n  its own discovery. No interpretations ('I think'), no assumptions ('probably').\"*\n\nRead literally, line 73 already flags the item's own AC #6 test case (*\"this is very likely\n`defect`\"*) as a violation \u2014 the word `likely` is in its prohibited list.\n\nSo the accurate problem statement is **not** \"the rule is missing\" but:\n\n> The rule exists in `agent-orchestration`, but no development-harness grooming or close workflow\n> loads, references, or restates it, so dh orchestrators author dispatch prompts with no governing\n> discipline in context \u2014 and dh's own two contract skills say nothing about prompt content.\n\nThat distinction materially changes the desired outcome (\u00a72) and the solution space, and it should\nbe resolved before planning rather than carried forward as written.\n\n### 1.5 Where dh's own contract surfaces stand today\n\n- `skills/subagent-contract/SKILL.md` (44 lines) is written entirely in second person to the\n  **dispatched agent** \u2014 status line, result destination, reporting. It is loaded via each agent's\n  `skills:` frontmatter, so an orchestrator never reads it. A rule addressed to orchestrators placed\n  here would be delivered to the wrong party.\n- `skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md` is the **orchestrator-facing** counterpart \u2014 \"which agent a dh\n  workflow dispatches\". `AGENTS.md:266-268` states the design principle explicitly: *\"Dispatch\n  behaviour is defined in the `dispatch-contract` skill \u2026 so the decision belongs there and nowhere\n  else.\"*\n- `skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` references **neither** contract skill and no\n  agent-orchestration delegation skill. This is the concrete gap: the groom orchestrator's only\n  dispatch guidance is `swarm.md` itself.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Desired Outcome\n\nExpressed as observable behaviour, not mechanism:\n\n1. **One answer per question.** An orchestrator preparing any dh dispatch can find a single\n   governing statement of what a dispatch prompt carries, and it does not conflict with the\n   statement any other dh document makes.\n2. **Capability implies responsibility.** An agent that can reach the item's content fetches it\n   itself; the orchestrator supplies the reference. Content already addressable by a section name is\n   never re-typed into a prompt.\n3. **The claim-scoped exception is stated, not accidental.** `fact-checker` is dispatched per-claim,\n   not per-item; its pasted claim is legitimately dynamic. Whatever rule emerges must distinguish\n   *\"content retrievable by reference\"* from *\"content that exists only because this run produced\n   it\"* \u2014 a blanket \"always self-fetch\" would be wrong for it.\n4. **Speculative framing is out of bounds and checkable.** An orchestrator does not write a\n   conclusion, verdict, or hedged guess into a prompt for an agent whose value is independent\n   judgement \u2014 and the governing text is specific enough that a reader can hold the classifier\n   incident's actual words against it and get a clear verdict.\n5. **`swarm.md` stops contradicting itself.** Its prose and its sequence diagram describe the same\n   groomer contract.\n6. **The four self-fetching teammates are unchanged.** Their existing wording is the reference\n   behaviour, not something to be re-litigated.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Stakeholders\n\n| Stakeholder | Stake |\n|---|---|\n| **Groom orchestrator** (`work-backlog-item` groom flow) | Primary consumer. Today it reads a self-contradicting `swarm.md` and no dispatch discipline. |\n| **`backlog-item-groomer`** | Its `## Input` is the concrete pattern break; the largest orchestrator-authored prompt in the swarm feeds it. |\n| **`fact-checker`** | The legitimate exception. At risk of being over-corrected by a blanket rule. Also receives orchestrator-chosen `PRIMARY_SOURCE` / `VERIFICATION_METHOD` \u2014 a second, unexamined anchoring surface. |\n| **Independent verifiers** (`classifier`, `alignment-analyst`, `fact-checker`) | Their output value depends entirely on not being pre-framed. |\n| **Close-workflow orchestrator** (`close/start.md` 5.6) | Second instance of the same shape, with `dh:task-worker`. |\n| **28 dh agents + 1 python3-development agent** declaring `dh:subagent-contract` | Any change to that skill reaches all of them. (Item text says 25 \u2014 measured count is 28 in `plugins/development-harness/agents/`.) |\n| **`dispatch-contract` consumers** (`implement-feature`, `multi-perspective-review`, `task-worker`, `alignment-analyst`, `review-synthesizer`) | Reached if the rule lands in `dispatch-contract` instead. |\n| **`plugins/agent-orchestration` maintainers** | Own the prior art. A dh-local restatement creates a second source of truth for one rule across two plugins. |\n| **`tests/test_subagent_contract_declaration_drift.py`** | Existing mechanical guard over contract declarations; establishes the precedent for what \"enforced\" means here. |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Risks\n\n**R1 \u2014 Duplicating a rule that already exists in another plugin.** (High likelihood, medium impact.)\n`agent-orchestration` already states both halves. Authoring an independent dh version creates two\ntexts that must be kept in agreement forever, and `plugin-creator:shared-content-references` in this\nrepo already warns against exactly this. Prior art must be reconciled, not ignored.\n\n**R2 \u2014 Placing an orchestrator rule in an agent-facing skill.** (Confirmed live in the item.)\nAC #3 and the Design Intent Alignment section both nominate `dh:subagent-contract` as the home. That\nskill is loaded by callees and speaks to callees; `AGENTS.md:266-268` says dispatch behaviour belongs\nin `dispatch-contract` \"and nowhere else\". Following the AC as written contradicts a documented\nprinciple and puts the rule where no orchestrator reads it.\n\n**R3 \u2014 Neither contract skill is loaded by the workflow that needs it.** Even correctly placed, a\nrule in `dispatch-contract` is not reached by the groom orchestrator, which loads no contract skill\nat all. Placement alone does not close the gap.\n\n**R4 \u2014 Over-generalising past the claim-scoped exception.** A rule phrased as \"agents with backlog\nMCP access self-fetch\" would, read literally, require `fact-checker` to self-fetch an item it is not\nscoped to. Its pasted claim block is correct behaviour.\n\n**R5 \u2014 The anchoring half is not mechanically checkable.** The self-fetch half is a static property\nof files and can be guarded the way `test_subagent_contract_declaration_drift.py` guards\ndeclarations. Speculative framing is a property of a prompt generated at runtime, which no test at\nrest can observe. Writing both halves into one \"checkable rule\" AC risks implying an enforcement\nsymmetry that cannot exist.\n\n**R6 \u2014 Content staleness is the concrete cost being under-counted.** Pasted content is a snapshot.\n`rtica-assessor.md:90-99` exists specifically because teammates write sections *after* earlier\nreads; the groomer, receiving pasted content, has no equivalent re-read instruction and can groom\nagainst a stale Impact Radius or RT-ICA.\n\n**R7 \u2014 The item's own evidence contains an uncorrected error that propagates.** The Fact-Check\nsection marks Claim 1 (*\"impact-analyst, fact-checker, classifier, and alignment-analyst each\ndeclare `## Input` as just `item_ref`/`selector`\"*) **VERIFIED** while citing evidence for only\nthree agents, and its Claim 2 note concedes *\"fact-checker differs in dispatch pattern\"* without\ndowngrading Claim 1. That un-downgraded verdict flowed into the Root-Cause Analysis, the\ndescription, and AC #5 as \"the other four self-fetch\". Planning from it would treat `fact-checker`\nas compliant when it is a second non-fetching agent, and would leave `rtica-assessor` (a genuine\nself-fetching teammate) out of the picture entirely. Notably, this is itself an instance of the\nanchoring failure the item describes: the orchestrator's framing survived a fact-check that\npartially contradicted it.\n\n**R8 \u2014 Scope creep from \"all 28 agents\".** The measured symptom is two agents and two workflow\ndocuments. Framing the fix around every `subagent-contract` consumer invites edits far beyond the\nevidence.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Open Questions\n\n**Q1 (blocking placement).** Given `AGENTS.md:266-268` \u2014 dispatch behaviour belongs in\n`dispatch-contract` \"and nowhere else\" \u2014 should the rule live in `dispatch-contract` rather than\n`subagent-contract` as AC #3 states? If `subagent-contract` is still intended, what reading of\nAGENTS.md permits it?\n\n**Q2 (blocking scope).** Given the rule already exists at\n`agent-orchestration/skills/agent-orchestration/SKILL.md:73,75-80` and\n`delegate/SKILL.md:134`, is the desired outcome (a) dh referencing the existing rule, (b) dh\nrestating a scoped subset, or (c) the existing rule being treated as insufficient for a stated\nreason? The item's premise that no rule exists is refuted; the answer changes what \"done\" means.\n\n**Q3 (blocking reachability).** How does the rule reach the groom orchestrator, which loads no\ncontract skill? Is the intended surface `work-backlog-item` gaining a skill load, or `swarm.md`\ncarrying the rule inline?\n\n**Q4 (correctness of the contract).** Is `fact-checker`'s pasted claim block accepted as correct\nbehaviour and named as such, or is it a third instance to fix? The item currently treats it as an\nexemplar of the pattern it does not follow.\n\n**Q5.** Does `rtica-assessor` belong in the item's inventory? It is a groom-swarm teammate that\nself-fetches (`:39`, `:92`) and is absent from the item's \"five agents / four self-fetch\" framing.\n\n**Q6.** Does the orchestrator's supply of `PRIMARY_SOURCE` and `VERIFICATION_METHOD` to\n`fact-checker` (`fact-checker.md:52-53`) count as pre-forming a verdict \u2014 the orchestrator choosing\nwhich source will confirm or refute \u2014 or as legitimate dispatch scope?\n\n**Q7 (enforcement expectation).** AC #3 and AC #6 require rule text \"specific enough to flag a\nconcrete violating example\". Is that satisfied by a human/agent reading the text, or is a mechanical\nguard expected? R5 says the anchoring half admits no test at rest.\n\n**Q8.** Should `swarm.md`'s internal contradiction (line 94 vs lines 269-272) be recorded as a\ndistinct defect? It is a self-contradicting instruction independent of where the rule lands.\n\n**Q9.** The item cites \"25 agents that load `dh:subagent-contract`\"; the measured count in\n`plugins/development-harness/agents/` is 28 (plus one in `python3-development`). Should the item's\nfigures be corrected before they are quoted into the plan?\n\n---\n\n## 6. Evidence Index\n\nAll paths relative to repo root; all read from the working tree on 2026-08-29.\n\n| Claim | Source |\n|---|---|\n| Groomer `## Input` names literal content fields | `plugins/development-harness/agents/backlog-item-groomer.md:48-53` |\n| Groomer has backlog MCP but zero `backlog_view` | `\u2026/agents/backlog-item-groomer.md:4`; grep: 0 hits |\n| fact-checker input is a pasted claim block | `\u2026/agents/fact-checker.md:45-55` |\n| fact-checker has backlog MCP but zero `backlog_view` | `\u2026/agents/fact-checker.md:4`; grep: 0 hits |\n| impact-analyst self-fetch | `\u2026/agents/impact-analyst.md:24,26` |\n| classifier self-fetch | `\u2026/agents/classifier.md:19,25` |\n| alignment-analyst self-fetch | `\u2026/agents/alignment-analyst.md:21,100` |\n| rtica-assessor self-fetch + re-read | `\u2026/agents/rtica-assessor.md:39-44,90-99` |\n| swarm.md paste-in list (11 fields) | `\u2026/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/swarm.md:269-272` |\n| swarm.md diagram says groomer self-reads | `\u2026/workflows/groom/swarm.md:94` |\n| swarm.md diagram pastes to four teammates | `\u2026/workflows/groom/swarm.md:65-68` |\n| close 5.6 re-types criteria into task-worker | `\u2026/workflows/close/start.md:107-121` |\n| subagent-contract is agent-facing, 44 lines | `\u2026/skills/subagent-contract/SKILL.md` |\n| dispatch-contract is the orchestrator-facing surface | `\u2026/skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md` |\n| \"dispatch behaviour \u2026 belongs there and nowhere else\" | `plugins/development-harness/AGENTS.md:266-268` |\n| work-backlog-item loads neither contract skill | grep of `\u2026/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md`: 0 hits |\n| Prior art \u2014 pass-through vs pre-gathering | `plugins/agent-orchestration/skills/agent-orchestration/SKILL.md:75-80` |\n| Prior art \u2014 \"likely\"/\"probably\"/\"seems\" prohibited | `plugins/agent-orchestration/skills/agent-orchestration/SKILL.md:73` |\n| Prior art \u2014 no pre-gathering, no interpretations | `plugins/agent-orchestration/skills/delegate/SKILL.md:134` |\n| SAM quote used in Design Intent Alignment is accurate | `plugins/development-harness/CONTEXT.md:96` |\n| 28 dh agents declare `dh:subagent-contract` | grep -rl over `plugins/development-harness/agents/` |\n| Existing mechanical guard precedent | `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_subagent_contract_declaration_drift.py` |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Post-Implementation Status (context-refinement, 2026-08-29)\n\nIndependently re-verified against the working tree after plan `Pb31107bf` (5 commits: `e5aa9b85a`,\n`8962e1589`, `620c6fcc6`, `ee23bb4b7`, `f35a454eb`) completed. All 6 plan acceptance criteria PASS.\n\n| Q/R | Resolution |\n|---|---|\n| Q1 | Resolved \u2014 rule split by audience; orchestrator half lives in `dispatch-contract`, per `AGENTS.md:268`. |\n| Q2 | Resolved as (b) \u2014 dh authored its own scoped `<dispatch_prompt_scope>` text rather than citing the `agent-orchestration` prior art. **R1 stays open as a result** (see below). |\n| Q3 | Resolved by design, not by skill-load wiring \u2014 `swarm.md` names `dh:dispatch-contract` in prose; `work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` frontmatter was deliberately left unchanged (scope-bounded, not an oversight). |\n| Q4 | Resolved \u2014 `fact-checker`'s pasted claim block is documented as the accepted exception in `subagent-contract/SKILL.md`'s `<dispatch_input>` clause. |\n| Q5 | Resolved \u2014 `rtica-assessor` added to the reference-pattern audit set. |\n| Q6 | Deferred, as planned \u2014 not addressed by this plan; still open for a follow-up item. |\n| Q7 | Deferred, as planned \u2014 no mechanical guard added; anti-speculation rule remains unenforced at rest. |\n| Q8 | Resolved \u2014 `swarm.md` prose and diagram now agree (fixed by the same edit). |\n| Q9 | Not addressed \u2014 the item's stale \"25 agents\" figure was not corrected anywhere; harmless but still inaccurate if re-quoted. |\n| R1 | **Still open.** The new `dispatch-contract` `<dispatch_prompt_scope>` section is an independent dh-local restatement, not a citation of `agent-orchestration/skills/agent-orchestration/SKILL.md:73,75-80` or `delegate/SKILL.md:134`. Two texts stating a similar rule across two plugins now exist and must be kept in agreement manually. Confirmed in the implementing task's own handoff notes, not remediated. |\n| R7 | Not retroactively fixed \u2014 the original item's Fact-Check section still shows the uncorrected Claim 1 verdict; only the plan's own audit (T6) captured the accurate five-agent picture. |\n\n**Gap in the registered `audit-report` artifact** (`doc-drift-audit-groom-swarm-dispatch-contract`,\nnot edited by this task \u2014 flagged here for whoever owns it): its \"Analyzed Files\" list and its\n\"four targeted commits\" summary omit `close/start.md` and commit `8962e1589` (T5, AC5) entirely. AC5\nwas independently re-verified here (`grep -c 'Parse each' close/start.md` \u2192 `0`) and passes, but the\nregistered audit never checked it.\n"}